From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: a733: PLL_GPU0's lock detector must be enabled with the PLL

`clk-sunxi-ng-a733-gpu-pll-module-clock-bus-gates-and-reset` gave pll_gpu0_clk `.lock = BIT(28)` so the framework waits for the PLL to
relock. It never asserts, because bit 28 is only meaningful while bit 29 is
set, and bit 29's reset default is Disable.

UM PLL_GPU0_CTRL_REG:

  | bit | field       | access | default |
  | 29  | LOCK_ENABLE | R/W    | 0x0 = Disable |
  | 28  | LOCK        | R      | 0 = Unlocked, 1 = Locked (stable) |

So ccu_helper_wait_for_lock() polls a bit that cannot become 1: it spins its
full 70 ms, WARN_ON()s the timeout, and the driver proceeds having confirmed
nothing about the PLL it just retuned.

MEASURED, not inferred. On the 8 GiB board with a test build (the 1008 MHz pin), every boot
carried:

  WARNING: CPU: 7 PID: 244 at drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu_common.c:38
           ccu_helper_wait_for_lock.part.0+0x8c/0xa4
    x2 : 00000000c9105300   <- this register as we program it
    x28: 000000003c14dc00   <- 1008000000, the rate being applied

0xc9105300 is bit 31 set, bit 29 CLEAR, bit 28 clear.

AND IT IS OURS, NOT THE SOC'S. Same register block, same boot, read on the
running board:

  0x02002020  0xF9005400   EN=1  LOCK_ENABLE=1  LOCK=1   (another PLL)
  0x020020e0  0xC9105300   EN=1  LOCK_ENABLE=0  LOCK=0   (PLL_GPU0)

That other PLL is brought up by the boot chain. PLL_GPU0 is the one PLL this
driver enables itself, which is exactly why it is the one that never got the
bit. No PLL in this file sets bit 29 today.

Setting the bit in .enable is how sunxi-ng expresses it: the enable mask is
written when the PLL is enabled, so the detector comes up with the PLL rather
than in a separate write that could race the relock.

FALSIFIER, one boot: PLL_GPU0 (0x020020e0) reads bit 29 = 1 AND bit 28 = 1, no
ccu_helper_wait_for_lock WARNING appears, and the GPU still renders
correctly at 1008 MHz. If bit 28 stays 0 with bit 29 set, the PLL genuinely is
not locking and the 1008 MHz pin needs revisiting, not this patch.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-sun60iw2.c
@@ -1588,8 +1588,40 @@
 #define SUN60IW2_PLL_GPU0_MIN_VCO	1272000000U
 #define SUN60IW2_PLL_GPU0_MAX_VCO	2520000000U
 
+/*
+ * ⚠ ENABLE CARRIES TWO BITS, AND THE SECOND ONE IS THE INSTRUMENT.
+ *
+ * PLL_EN is bit 31. Bit 29 is LOCK_ENABLE, R/W, and its reset default is
+ * DISABLE (UM PLL_GPU0_CTRL_REG);
+ * bit 28 LOCK is read-only and only
+ * ever asserts while the detector is on. So declaring .lock = BIT(28) without
+ * setting bit 29 asks ccu_helper_wait_for_lock() to poll a bit that cannot
+ * become 1 -- it spins its full 70 ms, WARN_ON()s, and the driver then
+ * proceeds having confirmed nothing.
+ *
+ * Measured on the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-08, a test build with the 1008 MHz pin: every rate
+ * change produced
+ *
+ *   WARNING: ... at drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu_common.c:38
+ *            ccu_helper_wait_for_lock.part.0+0x8c/0xa4
+ *
+ * with x2 = 0xc9105300, which is this register as we program it: bit 31 set,
+ * bit 29 CLEAR, bit 28 clear.
+ *
+ * That it is ours and not a property of the SoC was settled in the same
+ * register block on the same boot: another PLL at 0x02002020 reads
+ * 0xF9005400 -- enable, LOCK_ENABLE and LOCK all set. The boot chain brings
+ * that one up; PLL_GPU0 is the one PLL this driver enables itself, which is
+ * why it is the one missing the bit.
+ *
+ * ⚠ This is not cosmetic. `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-pll-gpu0-must-be-running-before-the-gpu-binds` exists because a PLL_GPU0 relock under the
+ * wrong conditions kills this SoC without printing anything, and lock
+ * detection is the only thing that can observe a relock at all. Rendering was
+ * byte-exact at 1008 MHz without it -- which is precisely the problem: the
+ * absence of the check is invisible until the day it is not.
+ */
 static struct ccu_nm pll_gpu0_clk = {
-	.enable		= BIT(31),
+	.enable		= BIT(31) | BIT(29),
 	.lock		= BIT(28),
 	.n		= _SUNXI_CCU_MULT_OFFSET_MIN_MAX(8, 8, 1, 12, 255),
 	.m		= _SUNXI_CCU_DIV(1, 1),
